WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:04.410
Welcome back to Curious Archive.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.060
This video is the second part of a series
exploring the incredible speculative evolution

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.580
project of Serina, the world of birds.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.390
Serina was created by Dylan Bajda — an exceptional
artist and worldbuilder.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:19.730
I have links below where you can follow and
support his work.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.670
When we left off, Serina was just beginning
to warm at the end of the Cryocene Era.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:30.519
Now, it is the Thermocene, a hothouse world
of rising sea levels and increasing volcanic

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:36.040
activity that has led to some extinctions
— especially in the warming oceans, where

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.250
large animals like the bloons have sadly died
out.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.239
In much of the world, however, newly evolved
species are enjoying this new climate.

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:49.399
100 million years into this grand experiment,
birds have evolved to fill virtually every

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:51.460
niche this moon has to offer.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.500
In a warm forest, a descendent of the Serilopes
of the Cryocene ambles through the woods.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.920
Their feathers have become coats of short
fuzz, and their beaks have given way to fleshy

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.920
snouts.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.220
Indeed, these creatures look quite a bit like
the non-avian dinosaurs of old.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:10.960
In size and behavior, however, the animals
are almost analogous to a deer on earth.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.840
Elsewhere in the warm forests, the Serilopes
have become the more specialized, horned omnivores

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.530
known as the Ornitheres.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:22.150
The beaks of these animals are now little
more than incisors within an almost mammalian

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:23.150
looking snout.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:28.470
And in the canopy above, the flightless, monkey-like
Mucks swing from the branches: which despite

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.760
their size, are actually an offshoot of the
massive Cryocene serestriders.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:37.720
But the larger serestriders are still with
us in the Thermocene, just changed since we

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:38.970
last saw them.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:44.000
Pachyderm Serestriders are huge, armored titans
that have traded in their feathers for defensive

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.000
spikes.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.290
Later in the Thermocene, some Serestriders
would specialize further, becoming heavily

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.110
armored goliaths called cragbacks.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:58.070
These bony plates help the animals deter large
predators, primarily tyrant serins, bone-crushing

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:00.850
hunters who have also endured beyond the Cryocene.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.880
In this age of dinosaur-like megafauna, one
might be paying little attention to the achievements

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.310
of the mudwickets, which are now called the
tribbits, and have become truly terrestrial

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.310
fish.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:15.160
Fully independent from the water, the tribbits
have spread out into the jungles, with their

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.670
tails specializing into a fully-functional
foot.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.840
This strange, tripod-like walking strategy
isn’t perhaps the most efficient — but

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.790
evolution is blind and the tribbits are making
it work.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:27.790
So stay tuned.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:32.940
Another easily overlooked group from the later
Thermocene are the Bumblets, small, quadrupedal

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:33.940
burrowers.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.950
At only about seven inches in length, these
tiny tunnelers don’t appear destined for

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:41.900
greatness, but times will soon change.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:47.700
During the Thermocene, the waters — largely
empty of megafauna due to Cryocene extinctions—now

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.730
represent a unique opportunity for any species
brave enough to venture into the depths.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.880
Gigaducks are a type of serestriders that
have become semi-aquatic, adopting the webbed

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.880
feet and lake-paddling lifestyle of ducks
on earth — just on a much larger scale as

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.280
gigaducks weight up to a ton.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.400
Since newborn gigaduck have trouble foraging
in deep waters, mother gigaducks care for

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:13.819
their chicks during their development: often
carrying them on their backs.

00:03:13.819 --> 00:03:18.959
In deeper waters, you can find the Porpants:
a highly varied classification of aquatic

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:19.959
birds.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:23.870
These sleek swimmers evolved later than the
bloons, which is why they weren’t wiped

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.650
out by the cryocene extinctions.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:31.100
The over-all body-shape and flipper derived
fins make some porpins resemble penguins — whereas

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.340
the larger ones seem similar to a porpoise
or dolphin.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.640
In the depths of the oceans, the swordwhales
now flourish — gigantic descendants of Cryocene

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:41.640
Swordsharks.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.630
These giants now have larger mouths that allow
them to feed upon vast school of plankton

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.030
and reach incredible sizes.

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:54.230
While these swordwhales are gentle giants,
the Gigadon, a variant of swordwhales, are

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.780
decadently not.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:04:01.440
These orca-whale sized predators don’t just
possess sharp jaws, but unusually keen minds

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.440
for a fish.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.270
They use these large brains to coordinate
pack hunting, aided by the reflective markings

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:13.700
on their skin that they can use — incredibly
— as a form of basic communication.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.370
Less intelligent, and far less elegant looking,
we have the Lumphead Grumpus — a creature

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.769
that might have the best name of anything
on Serina.

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:26.599
These slow-moving mollusk-eaters have evolved
elongated fin-like structures on their faces

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:30.070
that help them navigate the pitch-black waters
where they search for food.

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.860
A very unique-looking species indeed.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.639
Leaving the waters, the isolated Kryan islands
have also changed quite a bit since our last

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:38.639
visit.

00:04:38.639 --> 00:04:42.860
The islands themselves are now smaller due
to erosion and rising sea-levels, and have

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.960
become more tropical in climate.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.009
And the species populating these islands have
also become far stranger.

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:54.870
Instead of snufallos and butcher raptors,
you can now find creatures like the elefinches

00:04:54.870 --> 00:05:01.020
— truly unique looking birds with two tentacle-like
appendages that can grasp food and other objects.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.410
How did something like this possibly evolve
from a bill?

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.129
Well, elefinches are descended from burrowers
whose bills became fleshier and more flexible

00:05:09.129 --> 00:05:11.819
to aid with rooting through the dirt for food.

00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:16.790
Over millions of years, these bills became
softer and more muscular — eventually splitting

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:19.479
into two distinct, trunk-like apparatuses.

00:05:19.479 --> 00:05:23.510
And so, one of the strangest creatures on
Serina emerged.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:29.240
The perhaps somewhat less impressive cousins
of elefinches on these islands are the snuffles,

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:34.409
small, soft-snouted nocturnal birds with sensitive
whiskers that aid in their night-time hunts

00:05:34.409 --> 00:05:35.699
for food.

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:40.229
One offshoot of this group, the water snuffles,
has something even stranger than whiskers

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:45.360
sprouting from the ends of its bill — fleshy,
finger-like appendages similar in some ways

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.099
to the elefinches trunk.

00:05:47.099 --> 00:05:49.270
A really unique looking creature.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.879
And in the midst of this incredible peak of
biodiversity —on the Kryan Islands, the

00:05:53.879 --> 00:05:58.370
oceans, and the mainlands — the Tribbets,
the terrestrial descendants of the mudwicket

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:04.139
fishes, have in the late Thermocene become
strange, three-legged organisms called hoppers.

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:07.869
The adaptation of these unusual animals into
land-dwellers has been a true achievement,

00:06:07.869 --> 00:06:10.930
and they’re now thriving in the warm temperatures.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:14.240
Still, the hoppers are about the size of a
rat.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:19.469
It would take a mass extinction for them to
become a dominant species on Serina.

00:06:19.469 --> 00:06:22.039
…Which is exactly what happened next.

00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:28.719
A rise in global temperatures, volcanic fumes,
eruptions, and ocean anoxia creates a runaway

00:06:28.719 --> 00:06:33.659
effect that results in the worst mass extinction
event in the moon’s history.

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:40.090
Simply called, ‘Armageddon,’ the end-Thermocene
extinction would obliterate over 99% of all

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:41.909
life on Serina.

00:06:41.909 --> 00:06:44.789
But this was not the end…

00:06:44.789 --> 00:06:49.009
Eventually, Armageddon would pass, and the
sun would rise on the Pangeacene.

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:54.720
It is now ten million years after the mass
extinction, and the fire and brimstone are

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:57.699
already a distant memory.

00:06:57.699 --> 00:07:03.689
In this era, all of Serina's major landmasses
have come together to form a single supercontinent.

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:08.270
The climate has somewhat stabilized, and the
survivors of ‘Armageddon’ are beginning

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.919
to re-diversify.

00:07:09.919 --> 00:07:14.759
But the inheritors of this new world are quite
different from its old monarchs.

00:07:14.759 --> 00:07:19.960
If one explores the continental forests of
this reborn moon, one would find far stranger

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.500
life than ever before.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:29.029
Lumbering among the tress comes a terrestrial
muck, a descendant of their smaller ancestors.

00:07:29.029 --> 00:07:33.629
These hook-clawed creatures might look odd,
but they’re far from the strangest new forms

00:07:33.629 --> 00:07:34.999
of life here.

00:07:34.999 --> 00:07:40.319
From the water comes a descendant of the water-snuffle,
one of the only species from the Kyran islands

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:43.289
to survive, thanks to its aquatic adaptations.

00:07:43.289 --> 00:07:47.520
The finger like tendrils on the ends of their
beaks are becoming more pronounced, and may

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.339
soon become more complex.

00:07:50.339 --> 00:07:56.189
Out of a riverbank erupts a bumblet, the sole
survivor out of all the vivas that for millions

00:07:56.189 --> 00:07:57.869
of years dominated the planet.

00:07:57.869 --> 00:08:02.749
It’s fascinating that out of all its grand
cousins, thanks to its burrowing lifestyle,

00:08:02.749 --> 00:08:08.160
the bumblet is now the sole representative
of their evolutionary line. ….and at the

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:16.009
edge of the forest, a tribbethere, the much-changed
descendent of the mudwickets, stalks for prey.

00:08:16.009 --> 00:08:21.149
They’ve become a muscular, dog-like carnivore,
with a powerful hind leg that allows them

00:08:21.149 --> 00:08:23.599
to make great lunges at prey.

00:08:23.599 --> 00:08:28.529
Once at the bottom of Serina’s food chain,
their time has now come…

00:08:28.529 --> 00:08:33.050
Over the next several million years, through
the early and middle Pangeacene, members of

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:37.440
this group called the Canitheres would prove
to be the most successful and prominent large

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.500
predators on Serina.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:44.420
The author writes that the predators' flexible
spine, which moves in an up and down arc,

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:49.430
works almost like a spring, collecting energy
as the creature contracts its legs and releasing

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:53.060
it as it extends, making for an efficient
running gait.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:58.620
It’s hard to imagine that these warm-blooded,
furry organisms were once fish brought in

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.209
to be the canaries’ food source.

00:09:01.209 --> 00:09:06.100
Another descendent of the mudwickets has taken
to the trees, becoming the somewhat alarming

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.709
looking handfish.

00:09:07.709 --> 00:09:11.889
These creatures look like a cross between
a monkey, a frog, and a spider, with long

00:09:11.889 --> 00:09:16.450
fraktaline digits well suited to climbing
and holding onto branches.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:21.910
Later in the pangeacene, the mudwicket descendants
would even conquer the air, with the Tribbats

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:26.630
now taking to the skies alongside the birds
— the first group of vertebrates on Serina

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.990
to independently develop powered flight.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:33.509
While their wing design isn’t quite as efficient
as birds in the open sky, Tribbats are the

00:09:33.509 --> 00:09:36.740
most common animals of the tropical forests.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.680
But the birds are not down for the count just
yet.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:44.720
Indeed, several million years into the pangeacene,
flying birds have managed to reach sizes never

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:46.790
before seen in their history.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:52.270
These majestic giants are called Archangels,
the descendants of small, tree-dwelling ancestors

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:57.800
who outlasted the Thermocene extinction and
then rapidly grew to sizes rivaling the largest

00:09:57.800 --> 00:09:58.980
pterosaurs.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:04.060
Scampering nearby on the open plains are herds
of serezells — a descendant of the same

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:10.050
avian Armageddon survivor as the archangels,
though serezells have become swift-footed

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.050
grazers.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:15.310
They are marked with bold, contrasting patterns
of stripes and spots, which likely serve to

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:21.149
hide their outlines when moving in large groups,
just as is the case for Zebra on our own planet.

00:10:21.149 --> 00:10:24.829
But the serezells dominance on the plains
won’t last.

00:10:24.829 --> 00:10:29.930
In the later Pangeacene, they’ll be edged
out by the Circuagodonts — a highly specialized

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:35.050
group of tribbetheres that are unrecognizable
compared to their fish ancestors.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:39.790
The Circuagodonts possess specialized teeth
that allow them to eat a wider variety of

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:45.990
grasses than Serezells — allowing them to
usurp the serezells’ place on the plains.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:51.830
Indeed, beyond the pagneacene, it’s not
megafauna like the Serezelles or Archangles

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:55.330
that will contribute most to the legacy of
birds on Serina.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:11:00.529
In time, the humble bumblets will move above
ground, becoming a group of foragers called

00:11:00.529 --> 00:11:01.529
bumblebadgers.

00:11:01.529 --> 00:11:05.480
And in the game of life, they’re a new player
to keep an eye on.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:10.220
Also furthering the legacy of birds, though
taking it in an admittedly strange direction,

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:13.070
are the excellently-named ‘mittens.’

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:17.480
These descendants of the water-snuffle have
developed a mobile set of tentacles on their

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:20.730
snouts which they use to manipulate food items.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:25.790
The somewhat Cthulhu-looking creatures are
quite intelligent, and are starting to use

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:27.470
basic tools…

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:33.509
In time, the mittens branch off into the plains-dwelling
bludgebirds, animals that use crude weapons

00:11:33.509 --> 00:11:35.149
to aid in hunting.

00:11:35.149 --> 00:11:40.470
These creatures are clearly highly intelligent,
perhaps even one of the smartest birds ever

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:41.860
to exist on Serina.

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:46.829
However, despite appearances, they’re not
actually smarter than say, the crows or great

00:11:46.829 --> 00:11:48.139
apes of our world.

00:11:48.139 --> 00:11:54.820
Indeed, to find the first true example of
sapience on Serina, one must look elsewhere…

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:59.190
Watching the bludgebirds from the branches
are fork-tailed babbling jays.

00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:04.110
Unlike the bludgebird, this small species
is not extraordinary for its appearance.

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:09.339
Furthermore, they are a very young species,
having been in existence for no more than

00:12:09.339 --> 00:12:10.949
4,000 years.

00:12:10.949 --> 00:12:16.110
Yet babbling jays are the smartest creatures
yet to arise on Serina.

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:20.370
Possessing an incredible capacity for tool
crafting, language, and critical thinking,

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:25.569
babbering jays even produce art using bark
and stone and possess superstitions and a

00:12:25.569 --> 00:12:27.069
developing culture.

00:12:27.069 --> 00:12:30.670
Sadly, this brief glimmer of brilliance wouldn’t
last.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:35.170
In a relative blink of an eye, the harsh desert
environment that spurred the development of

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:40.300
their keen intellect would shift, and the
babbering jays would prove that no species,

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:44.060
no matter their intelligence, is immune to
extinction.

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:48.680
Looking forwards beyond the Pangeacene, it
is neither the babbering jays nor the bludgebirds

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:58.040
that will continue to bear the torch of intelligence,
but some of the most unexpected species…

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:02.410
Part three of this series will cover the rise
of true sapience on Serina.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:04.829
But for now, that’s all the time we have.

00:13:04.829 --> 00:13:09.000
If you enjoyed learning about Serina, consider
following and supporting Dylan Bajda using

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.269
the links below.

00:13:10.269 --> 00:13:12.470
Also, thanks for watching.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:17.029
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support and like, subscribe, and hit the notification

00:13:17.029 --> 00:13:20.040
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:21.279
See you in the next video.

